    "body": "AI-generated image\nBy Alexander Whiteman\n25 November 2026\nMaritime decarbonisation costs will ultimately be passed on to cargo owners under the present structuring of the proposed IMO Net-Zero Framework, according to the Federal Maritime Commission’s (FMC) chair Laura Di Bella.\nWith industry leaders backing her assessment, Ms Di Bella said the competitive nature of shipping would see additional compliance expenses eventually fed through to cargo owners, with ongoing regulatory uncertainty already forcing costs up.\nWhen asked precisely how much of the IMO Framework’s estimated cost – which could be anywhere from $50bn-$260bn – would be passed on to shippers, Ms Di Bella told The Loadstar, “a very large percentage of it”.\n“Shippers are hit with so many pass-through expenses. We’re constantly looking at all of the ways to tamp down any sort of inflationary measures, be it fuel surcharges or any surcharges, but what makes us think that this isn’t going to be treated the same way?” she explained.\nManaging director of OceanScore Albrecht Grell agreed that the financial burden would ultimately be shouldered by cargo owners, arguing that shipping companies would seek to recover additional expenses through their commercial arrangements.\n“Shipping is highly competitive – any cost will eventually be passed on to the cargo owners,” Mr Grell told The Loadstar , before warning that the redistribution of costs will not affect all shipping companies equally.\nHe said those able to adapt more effectively to the changing regulatory and operational environment would be able to pass on a higher share of their compliance expenses, adding “the profit pool will not change but be redistributed within the industry”.\nSimilarly, zero44 co-founder and managing director Friederike Hesse told The Loadstar uncertainty surrounding the regulatory framework is itself creating additional costs for the industry, as companies factor potential risks into their commercial decisions.\n“Uncertainty is a cost too, companies price in risk premiums, put their eggs in multiple baskets instead of focusing investments, add contractual buffers and spend more on legal and administrative work, and that ends up in freight rates,” Ms Hesse said.\nShe added that this financial burden is then distributed through charter contracts, with responsibility depending on which party controls the operational decisions affecting fuel consumption and emissions.\n“Whoever controls speed, route and fuel consumption pays for the emissions. Under time charters that is usually the charterer; under voyage charters it is priced into the freight. Cargo owners and consumers pay their share through rates and surcharges.”\nReiterating that the cost of transitioning could be anywhere between $50bn-$260bn, Ms Di Bella said there were clear questions as to whether the infrastructure required for it had been properly accounted for in discussions surrounding the framework.\nPointing to an infrastructure study commissioned by the IMO in April, which Ms Di Bella said has not yet been completed, she called for clearer understanding of the investment required before moving ahead.\n“There is no infrastructure to support many of these alternative fuels. LNG is probably the closest that we have. So, we need to know what is it going to cost to get there? And then we work backwards.”\nBut Ms Hesse warned delaying emissions reductions would not cut decarbonisation’s eventual costs and could make the transition more expensive by leaving companies with less time to prepare through investment in vessels, fuels and infrastructure.\n“Postponing cuts now does not make them disappear, it means much steeper targets later, at a point when companies will not have had time to prepare through early investment in ships, fuels and infrastructure,” she added.",
